    <article>
        <header class="mt-8 sm:mt-10">
            <p class="font-mono text-[11px] tracking-[0.16em] uppercase" style="color:#8B85F4">No 11 · Story</p>
            <h1 class="mt-4 font-display text-[30px] leading-[1.14] font-extrabold tracking-[-0.01em] text-pretty sm:text-[38px] md:text-[44px] md:leading-[1.1]">From Couch To Marathon: Tracking My Training</h1>
            <div class="flex flex-col gap-2 text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-muted mt-4">
    <p>By <a href="https://habitkit.app/about" rel="author" class="font-semibold text-silver hover:text-accent focus-ring">Sebastian Röhl</a>, creator of HabitKit</p>
    <p class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-3 gap-y-1">
        <span>4 min read</span>
        <span>Published <time datetime="2024-07-24">Jul 2024</time></span>
                    <span>Updated <time datetime="2026-09-14">Sep 2026</time></span>
                    </p>
</div>
        </header>

        <div class="help-body guide-lead mt-8 sm:mt-9"><p>I used one habit called <strong>Sport</strong> to record running and strength training during roughly ten months of preparation for my first marathon. The grid helped me see whether I was showing up across weeks, including periods when illness interrupted training.</p>
<p>I am Sebastian, the creator of HabitKit. This is my own training record and experience with the app I build, not a training plan or evidence that using a tracker improves race performance.</p>
</div>

