                    <section id="the-goal-i-was-preparing-for" data-article-section class="mt-10 scroll-mt-4 sm:mt-11">
    
    <h2 class="font-display text-[20px] font-extrabold text-pretty sm:text-[22px]">The Goal I Was Preparing For</h2>

    <div class="help-body mt-[18px]"><p>As I approached my thirtieth birthday, I wanted to do something I had spent years putting off. Much of my twenties had been spent gaming and staying at home. My target was the Westenergie Marathon in Essen, Germany, on 15 October 2023.</p>
<p>I had around ten months to prepare. The question I used HabitKit for was straightforward: had I done a session today? The record gave me a view across the training block instead of leaving me to remember how regular the last few weeks had been.</p>
</div>
</section>
                    <section id="my-habitkit-setup" data-article-section class="mt-10 scroll-mt-4 sm:mt-11">
    
    <h2 class="font-display text-[20px] font-extrabold text-pretty sm:text-[22px]">My HabitKit Setup</h2>

    <div class="help-body mt-[18px]"><p>I kept running and strength work together in one habit:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Name:</strong> Sport</li>
<li><strong>Description:</strong> Weightlifting, running or similar</li>
<li><strong>Streak goal:</strong> six times per week</li>
<li><strong>Reminders:</strong> Monday to Saturday at 07:00</li>
</ul>
<p>That was my personal target at the time, not a suggested starting schedule. A single habit meant that either a run or a strength session could fill the day's tile.</p>
<p><img src="https://habitkit.app/blog-assets/charcoal-marathon.webp" alt="My Sport habit grid from marathon preparation, combining running and strength sessions" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></p>
<p><em>My original Sport record, presented in the site's charcoal layout. A filled day could be running, weightlifting or a similar activity; it does not represent a particular distance or intensity.</em></p>
</div>
</section>
                    <section id="what-a-training-week-contained" data-article-section class="mt-10 scroll-mt-4 sm:mt-11">
    
    <h2 class="font-display text-[20px] font-extrabold text-pretty sm:text-[22px]">What A Training Week Contained</h2>

    <div class="help-body mt-[18px]"><p>My usual week included an interval run on Monday, a marathon-pace run on Wednesday and a longer run at the weekend. Other sessions included weightlifting and bodyweight training.</p>
<p>I found the growing grid motivating. During weeks when illness slowed me down, seeing the earlier work helped me keep the longer preparation period in view. A gap did not replace the sessions already recorded.</p>
</div>
</section>
                    <section id="what-the-grid-could-not-tell-me" data-article-section class="mt-10 scroll-mt-4 sm:mt-11">
    
    <h2 class="font-display text-[20px] font-extrabold text-pretty sm:text-[22px]">What The Grid Could Not Tell Me</h2>

    <div class="help-body mt-[18px]"><p>The same filled tile could stand for very different sessions. It could not tell me whether a run was appropriately paced, whether I was recovering well, or whether my training was enough for the race.</p>
<p>Combining activities made the record simple, but it also meant the Sport grid could not answer “How many days did I run?” without more detail. That is the tradeoff to consider when choosing one broad habit or separate habits for different activities.</p>
<p>The useful distinction is between a record of showing up and a plan for what to do. HabitKit gave me the first. <a href="/help/guides/a-streak-is-not-a-consistency-score">Reading streaks alongside a longer history</a> explains why both views matter.</p>
</div>
</section>
                    <section id="set-up-a-record-for-your-own-goal" data-article-section class="mt-10 scroll-mt-4 sm:mt-11">
    
    <h2 class="font-display text-[20px] font-extrabold text-pretty sm:text-[22px]">Set Up A Record For Your Own Goal</h2>

    <div class="help-body mt-[18px]"><p>Choose what you want a filled day to mean before choosing a target. For a reading habit, that might mean reading one page. For an existing exercise routine, it could mean completing the session you had planned.</p>
<ol>
<li>Create a <strong>Build A Habit</strong> and name the action.</li>
<li>Choose <strong>Yes / No</strong> if one check-in is enough. Use <strong>Count</strong> or <strong>Measure</strong> if quantities would answer a more useful question.</li>
<li>Set <strong>Streak Goal</strong> to a schedule that fits your own routine. A weekly target counts successful days, not a named set of weekdays.</li>
<li>Record what actually happened. Use a day note to explain a change or interruption if that helps you review it later.</li>
</ol>
<p>These instructions describe HabitKit 1.18.0. The controls have changed since my 2023 preparation; the original Sport record above is retained as it was.</p>
<p>The free tier supports four active habits and day notes. <a href="/help/getting-started/install-habitkit-and-create-your-first-habit">Create your first habit</a> covers the controls, and <a href="/help/habits-and-streaks/take-a-day-off-without-breaking-your-streak">planning days off</a> explains weekly goals. Choose the record that helps you make a decision, rather than copying my six-day target.</p>
</div>
</section>
        
        
        
        
